# Source: Wikipedia summary
# Topic: Matter
T:Matter
T:Reference
T:Category
T:Science
T:------------
T:What it is
T:In physical
T:science,
T:matter is
T:anything
T:that has
T:mass and
T:occupies
T:space by
T:having
T:volume. All
T:everyday
T:objects that
T:can be
T:touched are
T:ultimately
T:composed of
T:atoms, which
T:are made up
T:of
T:interacting
T:subatomic
T:particles.
T:In everyday
T:as well as
T:scientific
T:usage,
T:matter
T:generally
T:includes
T:atoms and
T:anything
T:made up of
T:them, and
T:any
T:particles
T:that act as
T:if they have
T:both rest
T:mass and
T:volume.
T:However it
T:does not
T:include
T:massless
T:particles
T:such as
T:photons, or
T:other energy
T:phenomena or
T:waves such
T:as light or
T:heat. Matter
T:exists in
T:various
T:states.
T:These
T:include
T:classical
T:everyday
T:phases such
T:as solid,
T:liquid, and
T:gas – for
T:example
T:water exists
T:as ice,
T:liquid
T:water, and
T:gaseous
T:steam – but
T:other states
T:are
T:possible,
T:including
T:plasma,
T:Bose–Einstei
T:condensates,
T:fermionic
T:condensates,
T:and
T:quark–gluon
T:plasma.
